-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Input Resolution vs Window Size:** If the input spatial resolution ($H, W$) is smaller than or equal to the window size, the shift size is forced to $0$ and the window size is reduced to the minimum input dimension to avoid invalid partitioning [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Padding:** Feature maps must be padded to multiples of the window size before partitioning to ensure all pixels are covered by a window [S1, unofficial].
+## Subtopics
+
+### Swin Transformer
+A hierarchical vision transformer using shifted windows. DuDoTrans adapts this for 2D tomographic slices. Choose when long-range dependencies are critical but full attention is too expensive. [Link to Swin Transformer article if available, otherwise generic link].
+
+### Window-Based Multi-Head Self-Attention (W-MSA)
+The core attention mechanism in DuDoTrans. It restricts attention to local windows. Choose when computational efficiency is needed for high-resolution images.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Complexity:** Standard Multi-Head Self-Attention has quadratic complexity $O(N^2)$ with respect to the number of tokens $N$. DuDoTrans uses Window Attention (W-MSA), restricting attention to local windows of size $W_s$. This reduces complexity to $O(N \cdot W_s^2)$, making it more efficient for high-resolution images [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** The method is designed for sparse-view CT reconstruction, where traditional methods suffer from artifacts. The dual-domain approach leverages the transformer's ability to model long-range dependencies within local windows and across shifted windows [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Effective recovery of fine details from limited projections; reduced computational cost compared to global attention.
-*   **Limitations:** Performance depends on the quality of the training data; may struggle with out-of-distribution inputs; requires significant GPU memory for large batch sizes or high resolutions due to the transformer architecture [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot W_s^2)$ per window, where $N$ is the number of tokens and $W_s$ is the window size. This is significantly faster than global self-attention $O(N^2)$ for large images [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the number of parameters in the transformer blocks and the size of the `relative_position_bias_table`.
+*   **Strengths:** Efficiently captures local and global context through stacked windows; robust to sparse-view artifacts due to strong inductive biases from the transformer architecture.
+*   **Limitations:** Performance depends heavily on window size selection; may struggle with very large structures spanning multiple windows if not properly aggregated.
+*   **When to use:** Sparse-view CT reconstruction where computational resources are limited but high-quality reconstruction is required.
+*   **When not to use:** Dense-view CT where simpler, faster methods like FBP or standard CNNs might suffice; or when real-time inference is critical and transformer overhead is prohibitive.
 
 ## Background
-DuDoTrans was introduced by Ce Wang, Kun Shang, Haimiao Zhang, Qian Li, and S. Kevin Zhou in the paper "DuDoTrans: Dual-Domain Transformer for Sparse-View CT Reconstruction," published in the International Workshop on Machine Learning for Medical Image Reconstruction in 2022 [S1, unofficial]. It adapts the Swin Transformer architecture, originally designed for natural image processing, to the domain of tomographic reconstruction, specifically targeting the challenges of sparse-view scenarios [S1, unofficial].
+DuDoTrans was introduced by Ce Wang, Kun Shang, Haimiao Zhang, Qian Li, and S. Kevin Zhou in the paper "DuDoTrans: Dual-Domain Transformer for Sparse-View CT Reconstruction," published in the International Workshop on Machine Learning for Medical Image Reconstruction in 2022 [S1, unofficial]. It builds upon the Swin Transformer architecture, adapting it for the specific challenges of tomographic reconstruction, such as handling sparse angular views and mitigating streak artifacts.
 
